<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Prescott Joule</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Joule</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Prescott Joule was an English physicist from Salford who studied the nature of heat and discovered its relationship to mechanical work. The son of a wealthy brewer, he managed the family brewery and pursued science as a serious hobby, discovering Joule's first law in 1841. In 1843 he estimated the mechanical equivalent of heat, a direct challenge to the caloric theory, and his work led to the law of conservation of energy and the first law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermodynamics">thermodynamics</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-kelvin">Lord Kelvin</a> he developed an absolute temperature scale. He died at home in Sale, and the SI unit of energy, the joule, is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-prescott-joule.jpg" alt="James Prescott Joule" width="330" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Roscoe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joule_James_sitting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joule was born in 1818 on New Bailey Street in Salford, the son of the wealthy brewer Benjamin Joule and Alice Prescott. He was tutored as a young man by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-dalton">John Dalton</a> and strongly influenced by the chemist William Henry and the Manchester engineers Peter Ewart and Eaton Hodgkinson. As a boy he was fascinated by electricity, and he and his brother experimented by giving electric shocks to each other and to the family's servants. As an adult he managed the brewery, and science was a serious hobby. Around 1840 he began to investigate whether the newly invented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electric-motor">electric motor</a> could replace the brewery's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engines</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>That question led to Joule's first law in 1841: the heat evolved by a voltaic current is proportional to the square of the current multiplied by the resistance. In 1843 he published results showing that the heating was generated in the conductor and not transferred from elsewhere, a direct challenge to the caloric theory, which held that heat could be neither created nor destroyed. He estimated the mechanical equivalent of heat at 4.1868 joules per calorie, but when he announced it at the British Association in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> in August 1843 he was met by silence.</p><p>He sought a purely mechanical demonstration, forcing water through a perforated cylinder, and then measured the heat produced in compressing a gas. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> rejected his paper of 20 June 1844, and he published it in the Philosophical Magazine in 1845. In June 1845 he reported his best-known experiment, a falling weight spinning a paddle wheel in an insulated barrel of water. A refined measurement of 1850 was closer to twentieth-century estimates. Hermann Helmholtz credited Joule and Julius Robert von Mayer in his declaration of the conservation of energy in 1847, and the young William Thomson, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-kelvin">Lord Kelvin</a>, was at first sceptical. From 1852 to 1856 Joule and Thomson collaborated, mostly by letter, and their discoveries included the Joule–Thomson effect.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Joule's work depended on extremely precise measurements; he claimed to measure temperatures to within a two-hundredth of a degree Fahrenheit, and was helped by the instrument-maker John Benjamin Dancer. He was elected a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1850 and received the Copley Medal in 1870. He married Amelia Grimes in 1847; she died in 1854. Joule died on 11 October 1889 at his home in Sale and is buried in Brooklands cemetery there. A memorial stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, though he is not buried there, and a statue by Alfred Gilbert is in Manchester Town Hall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Prescott Joule” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
